Wait for the PinMAME emulation thread to come up before probing its state.

PinmameRun() starts the emulation on a separate thread and returns immediately.
PinmameIsRunning() therefore still reports 0 (stopped) for a few milliseconds,
while the wait loop below only spins on 2 (starting). On slower hardware (Pi5)
the loop exits at once, the "(PinmameIsRunning() == 1)" test fails, and startup
is reported as failed even though the ROM loads and the machine runs fine a few
ms later:

    ERROR Failed to start emulation of rom 'mm_109c'
    INFO  [thread] PinMAME: osd_display_loading_rom_message(): loading mm_1_09c.bin...
    INFO  [thread] PinMAME: osd_create_display: 60.00 fps

The real damage is that m_onGameStartHandler is skipped, so the game start event
is broadcast with an empty game id. No DMD source ever gets registered, and the
ScoreView plugin finds no matching layout and renders nothing:

    WARN  PUP: Ignoring game start with empty gameId
    INFO  Serum: No colorization file found for
    INFO  VNI: No PAL file found for

Only wait when PinmameRun() itself reported success, so a genuine failure is
still reported without delay.

Signed-off-by: Adrien Beudin <beudbeud@gmail.com>

diff --git a/plugins/pinmame/Controller.cpp b/plugins/pinmame/Controller.cpp
--- a/plugins/pinmame/Controller.cpp
+++ b/plugins/pinmame/Controller.cpp
@@ -104,2 +104,10 @@
    PINMAME_STATUS status = PinmameRun(m_pPinmameGame->name);
+   // PinmameRun() is asynchronous: the emulation thread may not have flipped the
+   // state to 2 (starting) yet, so the loop below would exit immediately and the
+   // startup be reported as failed while it actually succeeds. Give the thread a
+   // chance to come up, bounded so a real failure is not delayed for long.
+   if (status == PINMAME_STATUS_OK)
+      for (int i = 0; (i < 20) && (PinmameIsRunning() == 0); ++i)
+         std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(75));
+
    while (PinmameIsRunning() == 2) // Wait until the machine is either running or stopped
